<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Company>MatterHackers Inc.</Company>
    <ReleaseVersion>2.20.12</ReleaseVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Remove="ObjParser\**" />
    <EmbeddedResource Remove="ObjParser\**" />
    <None Remove="ObjParser\**" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" Version="10.0.9" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The one and only boolean backend (see ManifoldKernel.cs). The C++ ManifoldNET
         engine it replaced is gone, and so is the managed CsgBySlicing fallback.

         Pure managed, so it carries no platform of its own: no runtimes/<rid>/native to
         probe on desktop, and nothing for a browser-wasm consumer to put on mono-wasm's
         emcc link line. That is what retired PolygonMesh/build/PolygonMeshBrowser.targets
         and the ManifoldRust NuGet package (0.5.0) it existed to tame - the C# port of
         manifold-rust replaces the P/Invoke binding over that project's Rust cdylib, and
         a browser head now needs no manifold machinery at all.

         WHAT THE SWAP CHANGED ON THIS ASSEMBLY'S SURFACE. Three members name a kernel type
         and so changed with it; the enum member SPELLINGS are identical on both sides, so a
         caller that reaches them through its own alias needs no edit, and one that wrote
         ManifoldRust.* by hand needs a one-line namespace change:

           - BooleanProcessing.DoArray and DoArrayAsync, whose optional `windingRule`
             parameter is ManifoldSharp.WindingRule rather than ManifoldRust.WindingRule
             (public);
           - MeshImportRejectedException.Status, now ManifoldSharp.Error rather than
             ManifoldRust.ManifoldStatus (internal, so not a break outside this assembly -
             recorded here because it is the same rename and would otherwise look like an
             unrelated change). Error's members carry the same names as ManifoldStatus's, so
             the text of every message built from one is unchanged.

         Nothing else in the public surface names a kernel type: MeshRepair, PartialBooleanException
         and SkippedBooleanOperand all speak in Mesh, string and int. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Submodules\manifold-sharp\ManifoldSharp\ManifoldSharp.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="MIConvexHull" Version="1.1.19.1019" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Same arrangement agg and RenderGl already use: lets the tests reach the kernel entry
         point directly, so a rejected input can be observed at the point it is rejected as
         well as through the public dispatcher. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Agg.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\agg\Agg.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\clipper_library\clipper_library.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DataConverters2D\DataConverters2D.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\geometry3Sharp\geometry3Sharp.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\QuadTree\QuadTree.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Triangle\Triangle.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\VectorMath\VectorMath.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Update="StyleCop.Analyzers" Version="1.1.118" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>